00:15:47.200 --> 00:15:49.600
and a computer processing the request,

00:15:49.600 --> 00:15:52.800
we have the ability to say who the client is

00:15:52.800 --> 00:15:54.200
and who the server is.

00:15:54.200 --> 00:15:59.200
So in this case, your phone is the client

00:15:59.200 --> 00:16:05.100
because it's the one sending the HTTP request here, right?

00:16:05.100 --> 00:16:08.800
And the backend computer, the one in the data center,

00:16:08.800 --> 00:16:12.400
is acting as the server.

00:16:12.400 --> 00:16:14.000
Cool, now we're starting to see

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:16.100
how the client makes requests to the server.

00:16:16.100 --> 00:16:20.600
What gets a little interesting is let's pretend that this server,

00:16:20.600 --> 00:16:25.600
all it has is video data locally.

00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:29.100
But the client actually asked for videos and comments.

00:16:29.100 --> 00:16:35.100
So what needs to happen now is there's probably some other server

00:16:35.100 --> 00:16:40.100
within, again, YouTube's backend.

00:16:40.100 --> 00:16:44.400
And that server has comments on it, right?

00:16:44.400 --> 00:16:51.600
It stores the comments locally on this server.

00:16:51.600 --> 00:16:55.000
So what can happen now is this first server

00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:58.600
that's trying to process this HTTP request, right?

00:16:58.600 --> 00:17:02.100
That asked for videos and comments.

00:17:02.100 --> 00:17:04.100
Well, it can sort the video, but it needs to grab some comments.

00:17:04.100 --> 00:17:09.200
So it's going to send its own HTTP request over to this other server.

00:17:09.200 --> 00:17:12.300
Say, hey, I need the comments for this video, right?

00:17:12.300 --> 00:17:14.500
Now, in this interaction, right,

00:17:14.500 --> 00:17:18.100
if we just look at this interaction over here on the right,

00:17:18.100 --> 00:17:24.900
in this case, this first computer is actually now acting as a client

00:17:24.900 --> 00:17:27.900
because it's sending an HTTP request.

00:17:27.900 --> 00:17:34.000
And this server over here is acting as a server, okay?

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:37.200
So this is an important example of when a computer is acting

00:17:37.200 --> 00:17:40.200
as both a server and a client, and that's totally okay.

00:17:40.200 --> 00:17:43.700
Okay, so this computer over here grabs the comments that were asked for

00:17:43.700 --> 00:17:49.200
and it sends them back in an HTTP response.

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:52.600
This first server, this server now has all the data it needs

00:17:52.600 --> 00:17:54.200
to respond to the client,

00:17:54.200 --> 00:18:00.100
and it can send a response back to the client.

00:18:00.100 --> 00:18:03.100
Hopefully, this has done a little bit to explain

00:18:03.100 --> 00:18:05.600
how clients and servers interact with each other

00:18:05.600 --> 00:18:07.100
and how really, at the end of the day,

00:18:07.100 --> 00:18:10.600
the word client and the word server only matter

00:18:10.600 --> 00:18:13.300
within the context of a single HTTP request.

00:18:13.300 --> 00:18:16.400
If a computer is sending an HTTP request,

00:18:16.400 --> 00:18:18.400
we consider it the client.

00:18:18.400 --> 00:18:21.500
If a computer is responding to an HTTP request,

00:18:21.500 --> 00:18:23.100
we consider it a server.

00:18:23.100 --> 00:18:25.900
And that's really all that client and server mean.

00:18:25.900 --> 00:18:29.300
So now we've got a quiz about clients and servers.

00:18:29.300 --> 00:18:30.300
It looks like the question is,

00:18:30.300 --> 00:18:33.900
does the example code run on a client or a server?

00:18:33.900 --> 00:18:37.300
And here's the example code written in JavaScript.

00:18:37.300 --> 00:18:42.500
And it looks like there's a URL here

00:18:42.500 --> 00:18:45.600
and a request is being made to that URL.

00:18:45.600 --> 00:18:47.300
So this has to be a client, right?

00:18:47.300 --> 00:18:51.000
Because a server doesn't need a URL to make a request to.

00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:52.600
Server serves the response.

00:18:52.600 --> 00:18:53.900
This is definitely a client.

00:18:53.900 --> 00:18:55.400
Okay, another client-server question.

00:18:55.400 --> 00:18:59.800
This one is, the computer responding to requests at the URL,

00:18:59.800 --> 00:19:04.000
https, api.boot.dev is a, well,

00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:06.800
if it's responding to requests, it must be a server.

00:19:06.800 --> 00:19:09.800
So we're finally ready to start talking about the Fetch API.

00:19:09.800 --> 00:19:11.200
We've already used it in a couple

00:19:11.200 --> 00:19:13.100
of the other coding challenges,

00:19:13.100 --> 00:19:15.200
but we haven't really explained what it is.

00:19:15.200 --> 00:19:19.500
In a nutshell, the Fetch API is a set of built-in functions

00:19:19.500 --> 00:19:22.700
and tools that we can use when we're writing JavaScript code

00:19:22.700 --> 00:19:25.200
in order to make HTTP requests.

00:19:25.200 --> 00:19:28.000
So an HTTP request has a lot going on with it.

00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:30.600
There's the URL that we have to send the request to.

00:19:30.600 --> 00:19:33.000
There's a body, there's some headers.

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:35.500
And at the end of the day, it would be a big nightmare

00:19:35.500 --> 00:19:37.800
if every time we had to write an HTTP request,

00:19:37.800 --> 00:19:40.200
we needed to write all of that logic from scratch.

00:19:40.200 --> 00:19:43.900
So the browser, in this case, provides that functionality

00:19:43.900 --> 00:19:47.000
through a built-in function called Fetch.

00:19:47.000 --> 00:19:49.500
And that's what we'll be using to make our HTTP requests

00:19:49.500 --> 00:19:50.600
in this course.

00:19:50.600 --> 00:19:52.900
Okay, let's look at how we can actually use

00:19:52.900 --> 00:19:55.100
the Fetch API in code.

00:19:55.100 --> 00:19:57.800
It all starts with this Fetch function.

00:19:57.800 --> 00:20:00.400
Now, this is not a function that we've defined in our code.

00:20:00.400 --> 00:20:03.700
It's a function that's made available to us by the browser.

00:20:03.700 --> 00:20:05.600
It's a built-in function.

00:20:05.600 --> 00:20:07.500
The way it works is it takes two inputs.

00:20:07.500 --> 00:20:11.600
It takes a URL, which is the URL that we want to make a request to,

00:20:11.600 --> 00:20:12.800
and a settings object.

00:20:12.800 --> 00:20:14.700
We'll talk more about the different ways

00:20:14.700 --> 00:20:18.900
we can configure an HTTP request in just a minute.

00:20:18.900 --> 00:20:22.100
After that, we actually do have to use this await keyword

00:20:22.100 --> 00:20:25.400
in front of the function call, in front of the fetch call.

00:20:25.400 --> 00:20:27.300
And that's because when we make a fetch call,

00:20:27.300 --> 00:20:29.700
we're quite literally sending bits of data,

00:20:29.700 --> 00:20:31.600
sometimes halfway across the world.

00:20:31.600 --> 00:20:36.900
So we need our code to pause and wait for that response to come back.

00:20:36.900 --> 00:20:38.300
It's going to take a while.

00:20:38.300 --> 00:20:40.700
And the await keyword allows us to do that.

00:20:40.700 --> 00:20:44.700
We'll go way more in depth about asynchronous programming

00:20:44.700 --> 00:20:47.400
and kind of what's going on under the hood here.

00:20:47.400 --> 00:20:50.000
But for now, just know that when you make a fetch request,

00:20:50.000 --> 00:20:51.700
you do need to await it.

00:20:51.700 --> 00:20:54.700
What we get back from all of that is the response object.

00:20:54.700 --> 00:20:57.200
The response object has a bunch of stuff in it

00:20:57.200 --> 00:20:59.400
that, again, we're going to get to.

00:20:59.400 --> 00:21:02.600
But for now, we just want kind of the meat of the response.

00:21:02.600 --> 00:21:04.000
We want the data, right?

00:21:04.000 --> 00:21:06.800
So we know that this server, in this case,

00:21:06.800 --> 00:21:09.300
the fantasy quest server that we're working with,

00:21:09.300 --> 00:21:11.500
responds with JSON data,

00:21:11.500 --> 00:21:15.000
which stands for JavaScript Object Notation.

00:21:15.000 --> 00:21:17.700
It's basically data that looks like this,

00:21:17.700 --> 00:21:22.200
kind of these brackets and colons and property value, right?

00:21:22.200 --> 00:21:24.400
Key value pairs.

00:21:24.400 --> 00:21:28.200
It is a JavaScript object, but in text form.

00:21:28.200 --> 00:21:33.400
So this.json method allows us to take that response body,

00:21:33.400 --> 00:21:37.000
which again is just text that looks like a JavaScript object,

00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:41.000
and parse it into an actual JavaScript object in our code

00:21:41.000 --> 00:21:42.800
so that we can work with it.

00:21:42.800 --> 00:21:45.800
And again, that method call, that.json method call,

00:21:45.800 --> 00:21:47.400
that needs to be awaited as well

00:21:47.400 --> 00:21:50.400
because it's an asynchronous operation.

00:21:50.400 --> 00:21:52.400
Once we're done with all of that,

00:21:52.400 --> 00:21:55.100
we have some response data.

00:21:55.100 --> 00:21:56.800
And that is a JavaScript object.

00:21:56.800 --> 00:22:00.200
Again, it's just a JavaScript object with key value pairs, right?

00:22:00.200 --> 00:22:05.200
Things like, you know, armor, five, name, lane.

00:22:05.200 --> 00:22:07.400
It's just very simple,

00:22:07.400 --> 00:22:11.300
simply structured JSON data that comes back from the server.

00:22:11.300 --> 00:22:13.900
So let's get to the assignment,

00:22:13.900 --> 00:22:15.800
is to fix the bug in the code.

00:22:15.800 --> 00:22:18.200
The problem is that we aren't waiting for the response

00:22:18.200 --> 00:22:20.800
to physically come back across the internet connection

00:22:20.800 --> 00:22:21.800
before continuing with our code.

00:22:21.800 --> 00:22:23.600
Okay, let's take a look.

00:22:23.600 --> 00:22:27.200
So it looks like down here, there's a get settings function.

00:22:27.200 --> 00:22:29.200
Looks like this is setting up the settings

00:22:29.200 --> 00:22:30.800
for the fetch request.

00:22:30.800 --> 00:22:34.400
This is a URL that we're gonna be making the fetch request to.

00:22:34.400 --> 00:22:36.400
It looks like there's some other authentication stuff,

00:22:36.400 --> 00:22:37.700
some logging.

00:22:37.700 --> 00:22:38.600
But really, at the end of the day,

00:22:38.600 --> 00:22:41.600
what we're interested in is this part here.

00:22:41.600 --> 00:22:45.300
So we're looking for a bug that we need to fix.

00:22:45.300 --> 00:22:47.600
And again, according to the assignment,

00:22:47.600 --> 00:22:49.700
it's because we're not waiting

00:22:49.700 --> 00:22:51.200
for the response to physically come back.

00:22:51.200 --> 00:22:54.100
So what happens if I just run the code right now?

00:22:54.100 --> 00:22:58.100
Type error, response.json is not a function.

00:22:58.100 --> 00:22:59.800
Well, that makes sense to me

00:22:59.800 --> 00:23:03.800
because this response object here is going to be garbage

00:23:03.800 --> 00:23:08.300
if we're not waiting for the fetch request to complete.

00:23:08.300 --> 00:23:11.500
Right, so we're trying to call this.json method

00:23:11.500 --> 00:23:14.400
on essentially a garbage object.

00:23:14.400 --> 00:23:19.000
So let's go ahead and await that fetch request.

00:23:19.000 --> 00:23:21.800
And there we go, we get all of our item data

00:23:21.800 --> 00:23:23.500
as we would expect.

00:23:23.500 --> 00:23:25.900
So we've already talked a lot about clients and servers

00:23:25.900 --> 00:23:29.200
and how clients make requests to servers.

00:23:29.200 --> 00:23:31.700
Well, this question is,

00:23:31.700 --> 00:23:35.000
the front end of a website is A

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:36.700
and the options are client and server.

00:23:36.700 --> 00:23:40.400
Well, the front end of a website

00:23:40.400 --> 00:23:41.500
can't really be a server.

00:23:41.500 --> 00:23:43.100
I mean, there are certain scenarios

00:23:43.100 --> 00:23:45.500
in which we could consider it a server,

00:23:45.500 --> 00:23:48.900
like very specific and rare scenarios.

00:23:48.900 --> 00:23:50.600
But generally speaking,

00:23:50.600 --> 00:23:52.500
the front end of a website is going to be a client

00:23:52.500 --> 00:23:56.300
because it's going to be making requests out to the backend.

00:23:56.300 --> 00:24:00.400
Okay, this question, a mobile phone is often used as an,

00:24:01.400 --> 00:24:04.100
and then the answers are HTTP server or HTTP client.

00:24:04.100 --> 00:24:08.200
Now, again, a phone could be a server.

00:24:08.200 --> 00:24:12.000
Like there are scenarios in which a phone could respond

00:24:12.000 --> 00:24:14.000
to an HTTP request, right?

00:24:14.000 --> 00:24:15.400
A phone is a computer.

00:24:15.400 --> 00:24:17.900
We could run some server software on it,

00:24:17.900 --> 00:24:19.900
but that would be very, very strange.

00:24:19.900 --> 00:24:21.800
Most of the time your phone,

00:24:21.800 --> 00:24:23.600
when you open it up and use an app on it,

00:24:23.600 --> 00:24:25.600
it's going to be making HTTP requests out

00:24:25.600 --> 00:24:27.400
to some backend server.

00:24:27.400 --> 00:24:32.200
So most often a mobile phone is an HTTP client.

00:24:33.700 --> 00:24:36.700
So let's talk just a little bit more about web servers.

00:24:36.700 --> 00:24:40.100
So most applications that you've probably written up

00:24:40.100 --> 00:24:43.600
until this point kind of start,

00:24:43.600 --> 00:24:47.100
do a bunch of stuff and then stop, right?

00:24:47.100 --> 00:24:48.900
Terminate when they're done doing what it is

00:24:48.900 --> 00:24:50.100
they're supposed to do.

00:24:50.100 --> 00:24:53.800
Servers are interesting in that they don't stop running,

00:24:53.800 --> 00:24:54.700
generally speaking, right?

00:24:54.700 --> 00:24:57.800
You turn on the server and then it just sits there

00:24:57.800 --> 00:25:02.100
on a computer listening for incoming HTTP requests.

00:25:02.100 --> 00:25:04.300
As they come in, it handles them, right?

00:25:04.300 --> 00:25:08.800
It sends responses, but it's just kind of always on.

00:25:08.800 --> 00:25:10.200
And again, this is important to understand

00:25:10.200 --> 00:25:13.300
because it's very likely that there's a lot of applications

00:25:13.300 --> 00:25:15.500
you've written up to this point that don't act that way, right?

00:25:15.500 --> 00:25:18.300
You run the code, the code exits.

00:25:18.300 --> 00:25:20.800
You run the code, the code exits, not with servers, right?

00:25:20.800 --> 00:25:22.700
With a server, you start up the server.

00:25:22.700 --> 00:25:24.600
And then in order to test your server code,

00:25:24.600 --> 00:25:28.000
you would actually need to send HTTP requests to it, right?

00:25:28.000 --> 00:25:30.400
Make sure that it behaves as you would expect

00:25:30.400 --> 00:25:33.700
given certain HTTP inputs.

00:25:33.700 --> 00:25:35.400
And then you'd actually need to probably manually

00:25:35.400 --> 00:25:36.600
stop your server from running

00:25:36.600 --> 00:25:39.900
because they're not designed to shut down automatically.

00:25:39.900 --> 00:25:41.400
So the question is,

00:25:41.400 --> 00:25:44.500
good web servers are listening for requests

00:25:44.500 --> 00:25:46.100
only when a client is active,

00:25:46.100 --> 00:25:48.600
only during business hours, or always?

00:25:48.600 --> 00:25:50.800
And the answer is always.

00:25:54.200 --> 00:25:56.600
Another question about servers here.

00:25:56.600 --> 00:26:00.900
So the question is, a server is the blank of an application

00:26:00.900 --> 00:26:03.600
and the options are backend and frontend.

00:26:03.600 --> 00:26:07.600
Well, a server isn't interacted with visually, right?

00:26:07.600 --> 00:26:09.100
So it can't be a frontend.

00:26:09.100 --> 00:26:11.100
So I'm gonna go with backend on this one.

00:26:11.100 --> 00:26:13.100
Okay, another question about servers.

00:26:13.100 --> 00:26:15.600
This time, it's actually a statement.

00:26:15.600 --> 00:26:18.100
It says, any computer can be a server,

00:26:18.100 --> 00:26:21.600
but the best servers are special made for serving data.

00:26:22.600 --> 00:26:23.600
Before I answer this question,

00:26:23.600 --> 00:26:25.100
I wanna just explain a little bit

00:26:25.100 --> 00:26:28.600
about kind of how the modern internet works.

00:26:28.600 --> 00:26:32.600
So generally speaking, new kind of web companies

00:26:32.600 --> 00:26:35.600
are spinning up their servers in data centers.

00:26:35.600 --> 00:26:39.100
Data centers owned by companies like Google Cloud Platform,

00:26:39.100 --> 00:26:41.600
Amazon Web Services, Azure.

00:26:41.600 --> 00:26:44.100
And the computers in those data centers

00:26:44.100 --> 00:26:47.600
are hyper optimized to be servers.

00:26:47.600 --> 00:26:50.100
They're optimized for server side workloads.

00:26:50.100 --> 00:26:52.100
That said, it's important to understand

00:26:52.100 --> 00:26:55.600
that realistically you can run server software

00:26:55.600 --> 00:26:58.100
on pretty much any computer, right?

00:26:58.100 --> 00:27:01.100
In fact, when we're developing our backend applications,

00:27:01.100 --> 00:27:03.600
even if we will eventually deploy them in a data center,

00:27:03.600 --> 00:27:07.600
we're generally developing them on our local machines.

00:27:07.600 --> 00:27:11.100
On just our work laptops.

00:27:11.100 --> 00:27:13.100
So the servers work perfectly fine on laptops,

00:27:13.100 --> 00:27:15.100
but it is important to understand

00:27:15.100 --> 00:27:19.100
that when the hardware is optimized for server workloads,

00:27:19.100 --> 00:27:21.100
you'll get more bang for your buck.

00:27:22.600 --> 00:27:26.100
So the answer here is going to be true.

00:27:26.100 --> 00:27:28.100
Any computer can be a server,

00:27:28.100 --> 00:27:30.600
but the best servers are made for serving data.

00:27:30.600 --> 00:27:32.600
Let's talk about web addresses.

00:27:32.600 --> 00:27:36.100
So I'm over here on my computer.

00:27:36.100 --> 00:27:40.100
And I want to communicate with, let's say,

00:27:40.100 --> 00:27:42.600
some server over here, right?

00:27:42.600 --> 00:27:44.600
Maybe I want to make a request to that server.

00:27:44.600 --> 00:27:47.100
Maybe I want to browse a website that's hosted there.

00:27:47.100 --> 00:27:49.100
Point is, I want to communicate with it.

00:27:49.100 --> 00:27:53.100
Trouble is, the internet is made up of much more

00:27:53.100 --> 00:27:55.600
than just my computer and the server, right?

00:27:55.600 --> 00:27:57.100
We have all these other devices

00:27:57.100 --> 00:27:58.600
that are connected to the internet.

00:27:58.600 --> 00:28:00.100
We have tons of other servers

00:28:00.100 --> 00:28:01.600
that are connected to the internet.

00:28:01.600 --> 00:28:09.100
So how can I tell the routing mechanisms of the internet

00:28:09.100 --> 00:28:15.600
that I want to communicate directly with that server?

00:28:15.600 --> 00:28:18.100
Well, that's where web addresses come in.

00:28:18.100 --> 00:28:24.100
And more specifically, what we call IP addresses

00:28:24.100 --> 00:28:26.600
or internet protocol addresses.

00:28:26.600 --> 00:28:29.100
So what does an IP address look like?

00:28:29.100 --> 00:28:31.100
Well, it looks something like this.

00:28:31.100 --> 00:28:37.600
8.13.156.7, OK?

00:28:37.600 --> 00:28:42.600
There's four sections separated by periods.

00:28:42.600 --> 00:28:47.600
And each section can be between 0 and 255.

00:28:47.600 --> 00:28:52.600
Four numbers, each number is one byte of information.

00:28:52.600 --> 00:28:57.100
This 8.13.156.7, this is just an example, right?

00:28:57.100 --> 00:28:59.100
It is a valid IP address,

00:28:59.100 --> 00:29:01.600
but I don't know if there's anything connected

00:29:01.600 --> 00:29:03.100
to this IP address.

00:29:03.100 --> 00:29:04.600
So just keep that in mind.

00:29:04.600 --> 00:29:08.100
There's many, many possible valid IP addresses.

00:29:08.100 --> 00:29:10.600
The only rule is that each section

00:29:10.600 --> 00:29:12.600
has a number between 0 and 255.

00:29:12.600 --> 00:29:15.100
So whenever you order a package online,

00:29:15.100 --> 00:29:18.600
you have a unique address for your home.

00:29:18.600 --> 00:29:22.100
And that's how the e-commerce store, say Amazon,

00:29:22.100 --> 00:29:25.600
knows how to send the package directly to your door

00:29:25.600 --> 00:29:28.100
and doesn't get mixed up anywhere else, right?

00:29:28.100 --> 00:29:30.600
IP addresses work the same way.

00:29:30.600 --> 00:29:32.600
Every device connected to the internet

00:29:32.600 --> 00:29:36.100
has its own unique IP address.

00:29:36.100 --> 00:29:37.600
So in this example from before,

00:29:37.600 --> 00:29:39.600
where I'm trying to communicate with the server,

00:29:39.600 --> 00:29:43.600
all I need to know is its unique IP address.

