It is important to understand that this format here,

00:29:49.100 --> 00:29:51.600
these four numbers separated by periods,

00:29:51.600 --> 00:29:55.100
this is just one format of an IP address.

00:29:55.100 --> 00:29:57.100
And it's actually the more popular format.

00:29:57.100 --> 00:29:59.600
It's called IPv4.

00:29:59.600 --> 00:30:02.600
But there is a newer format that's being used

00:30:02.600 --> 00:30:06.600
called IPv6 or IP version 6.

00:30:06.600 --> 00:30:09.100
And it looks similar,

00:30:09.100 --> 00:30:14.100
but there's actually way more possible IPv6 addresses

00:30:14.100 --> 00:30:15.600
than IPv4 address.

00:30:15.600 --> 00:30:21.600
And in IPv6, each section is separated by a colon,

00:30:21.600 --> 00:30:24.600
colon rather than a period.

00:30:24.600 --> 00:30:26.600
We can tell them apart, right?

00:30:26.600 --> 00:30:29.100
And there's actually one, two, three, four, five,

00:30:29.100 --> 00:30:35.100
six, seven, eight sections in an IPv6 address.

00:30:35.100 --> 00:30:38.100
And each section actually has more information in it.

00:30:38.100 --> 00:30:43.100
So the point is there's way more IPv6 addresses,

00:30:43.100 --> 00:30:46.600
way more possible IPv6 addresses out there

00:30:46.600 --> 00:30:48.600
than IPv4 addresses.

00:30:48.600 --> 00:30:49.600
And that's really important

00:30:49.600 --> 00:30:53.600
because we're actually running out of IPv4 addresses.

00:30:53.600 --> 00:30:57.600
There's really not that many combinations of numbers here.

00:30:57.600 --> 00:30:59.600
And so we're running into a problem

00:30:59.600 --> 00:31:02.600
where so many people and so many devices are online,

00:31:02.600 --> 00:31:06.600
and we're running short on these IPv4 addresses.

00:31:06.600 --> 00:31:11.600
So IPv6 has kind of given us more options.

00:31:11.600 --> 00:31:13.600
The same principle applies, right?

00:31:13.600 --> 00:31:16.600
The addresses are unique and uniquely identify

00:31:16.600 --> 00:31:17.600
machines on the internet,

