So let's say I have my web server over here, my server.

01:02:25.600 --> 01:02:29.600
And let's say I'm hosting a website.

01:02:29.600 --> 01:02:33.600
So I've got some piece of software that's hosting my website.

01:02:33.600 --> 01:02:36.600
Brought here like this.

01:02:36.600 --> 01:02:41.600
And I've got some customers that want to access my website.

01:02:41.600 --> 01:02:47.600
They'll be over here and they're sending HTTP requests.

01:02:47.600 --> 01:02:51.600
To my server so they can get access to my website.

01:02:51.600 --> 01:02:53.600
And that works just fine.

01:02:53.600 --> 01:03:03.600
The trouble is, what if I want to host multiple pieces of software on the same physical machine?

01:03:03.600 --> 01:03:11.600
Right. So let's say, for example, that in addition to my website, I also want to host a database.

01:03:11.600 --> 01:03:13.600
Maybe for my own purposes. Right.

01:03:13.600 --> 01:03:16.600
And it's not even public. Maybe it's just something that I use personally.

01:03:16.600 --> 01:03:20.600
But I want to be able to access it, say, from my home computer.

01:03:20.600 --> 01:03:23.600
This is me. I want to be able to get access to the database.

01:03:23.600 --> 01:03:32.600
The trouble is, now, how can my machine, my computer, know when a request comes in to my IP address?

01:03:32.600 --> 01:03:34.600
Right. To my computer.

01:03:34.600 --> 01:03:39.600
Whether the incoming request should be handled by my web server or by my database.

01:03:39.600 --> 01:03:41.600
That's where ports come in.

01:03:41.600 --> 01:03:48.600
Okay. So ports are virtual little hubs managed by the operating system.

01:03:48.600 --> 01:03:53.600
That allow us to segment incoming requests and incoming data streams.

01:03:53.600 --> 01:03:58.600
So now, if I have one host for my database.

01:03:58.600 --> 01:04:00.600
Sorry, one port for my database.

01:04:00.600 --> 01:04:02.600
And one port for my web server.

01:04:02.600 --> 01:04:07.600
And I can direct all my web traffic at that port for my web server.

01:04:07.600 --> 01:04:09.600
Let's just say that's port 80.

01:04:09.600 --> 01:04:16.600
And I can access my database on port, say, five, four, two.

01:04:16.600 --> 01:04:20.600
Those are pretty common ports for those use cases.

01:04:20.600 --> 01:04:28.600
So with ports, I can now actually run many different instances of different kinds of software.

01:04:28.600 --> 01:04:31.600
And network with them all at the same time.

01:04:31.600 --> 01:04:34.600
When I'm developing on my own machine, maybe I'm building a few different websites.

01:04:34.600 --> 01:04:40.600
I can actually run all of those websites connected to different ports at the same time.

01:04:40.600 --> 01:04:45.600
You can't bind two pieces of software to the same port.

01:04:45.600 --> 01:04:48.600
Again, for the reason we talked about at the beginning, right?

01:04:48.600 --> 01:04:54.600
The operating system wouldn't know which piece of software should handle which incoming network request.

01:04:54.600 --> 01:04:58.600
But we can run many pieces of software all bound to different ports.

01:04:58.600 --> 01:05:06.600
And your operating system actually allows for over 65,000 different ports at the same time.

01:05:06.600 --> 01:05:11.600
So there's not too much of a risk of running out of ports.

01:05:11.600 --> 01:05:15.600
So there are a ton of different ports that we can use when we are networking.

01:05:15.600 --> 01:05:23.600
And when we are navigating the web, we can optionally kind of type in the port, the URL, or we cannot.

01:05:23.600 --> 01:05:27.600
And if we don't, a default port will be used.

01:05:27.600 --> 01:05:33.600
So the question is, if a port is not present in a URL, the default is used based on the blank.

01:05:33.600 --> 01:05:37.600
The answers are domain, username, protocol, or domain name service.

01:05:37.600 --> 01:05:40.600
And the answer here is protocol.

01:05:40.600 --> 01:05:49.600
The default for the HTTP protocol is port 80, and the default for the HTTPS protocol is 443.

01:05:49.600 --> 01:05:51.600
The next question is a true-false question.

01:05:51.600 --> 01:05:57.600
And it says, only ports 80 and 443 can be used for HTTP and HTTPS traffic.

01:05:57.600 --> 01:05:59.600
That is false, right?

01:05:59.600 --> 01:06:07.600
Those are the defaults, but other ports could still be used optionally.

01:06:07.600 --> 01:06:13.600
I installed caddy on Mac by just typing brew install caddy.

01:06:13.600 --> 01:06:18.600
But if you're on a different operating system, head over to caddyserver.com.

01:06:18.600 --> 01:06:20.600
Just Google caddy, and I'm sure you'll find it.

01:06:20.600 --> 01:06:24.600
And you can find instructions for how to install it on your own machine.

01:06:24.600 --> 01:06:34.600
Once you have it installed, you're going to need to be on your command line in the same directory that has all these files we just created.

01:06:34.600 --> 01:06:41.600
I'm going to run the test directory here on my command line, and I'll run caddy file-server.

01:06:41.600 --> 01:06:47.600
Now, by default, the server is going to bind to port 80, which is the default HTTP port.

01:06:47.600 --> 01:06:53.600
And it's going to use localhost, which is just the domain of my own computer, right?

01:06:53.600 --> 01:06:58.600
So I'm connecting to my own computer on port 80.

01:06:58.600 --> 01:07:10.600
So when I go to localhost port 80 in my browser, which again is the default, so I don't really need to type port 80, I should get this rendered view of index.html.

01:07:10.600 --> 01:07:16.600
So you might be wondering how did caddy know to serve index.html, right?

01:07:16.600 --> 01:07:18.600
Why didn't it serve name.txt?

01:07:18.600 --> 01:07:32.600
Well, traditional file servers will typically route the path of a directory, which in this case would just be the root, which is slash, to a file called index.html in that directory, just by convention.

01:07:32.600 --> 01:07:43.600
For example, if I wanted to get name.txt, if I just wanted to get that text file, I could actually just go directly to name.txt, and I'd get that file.

01:07:43.600 --> 01:07:52.600
Caddy is taking the path slash name.txt and mapping it to slash name.txt on my file system.

01:07:52.600 --> 01:07:54.600
Hopefully that makes sense.

01:07:54.600 --> 01:08:00.600
Similarly, if I wanted to see this blog page, I could go to slash blog.

01:08:00.600 --> 01:08:14.600
And again, because blog is a directory, caddy is automatically redirecting that request from blog to blog index.html.

01:08:14.600 --> 01:08:19.600
If I were to type index.html, I'd get the same thing.

01:08:19.600 --> 01:08:22.600
So at this point, you may be thinking, well, that's great.

01:08:22.600 --> 01:08:27.600
So a URL path is just the same thing as a file system path once you've hooked up a web server.

01:08:27.600 --> 01:08:40.600
It's not exactly true. By convention, many static file servers just use the path to the file on your disk as the path in the URL.

01:08:40.600 --> 01:08:42.600
But it doesn't have to work that way.

01:08:42.600 --> 01:08:48.600
At the end of the day, the server software, in this case Caddy, can do whatever it wants.

01:08:48.600 --> 01:08:55.600
We could completely change how we parse the path of a URL and what we want to respond with.

01:08:55.600 --> 01:09:00.600
Many frameworks, back-end web frameworks, work this way.

01:09:00.600 --> 01:09:10.600
If you write a server in Go or in Django, there will often be a lot of custom logic that determines how different paths in a URL are handled

01:09:10.600 --> 01:09:15.600
and what files and data will be responded with when certain paths are accessed.

01:09:15.600 --> 01:09:22.600
So while it's common for a URL path to map directly to a file on a file system when we're talking about websites,

01:09:22.600 --> 01:09:26.600
when we're talking about web APIs, that's rarely the case.

