01:11:23.600 --> 01:11:25.600
Let's talk about query parameters.

01:11:25.600 --> 01:11:30.600
So while the path of an HTTP request typically changes something big,

01:11:30.600 --> 01:11:37.600
like the web page that you're looking at or the entire resource that you're requesting from the server, right?

01:11:37.600 --> 01:11:44.600
User data versus location data. A query parameter typically changes something very small about the request.

01:11:44.600 --> 01:11:49.600
Maybe some metadata, maybe a filtering option, something like that.

01:11:49.600 --> 01:11:53.600
At the end of the day, the server can do whatever it wants with the path or the query parameters,

01:11:53.600 --> 01:12:01.600
but kind of by convention, the query parameters tends to be smaller changes to the request.

01:12:01.600 --> 01:12:04.600
Maybe something like the options are changing, right?

01:12:04.600 --> 01:12:08.600
So, for example, we could look at how Google uses query parameters.

01:12:08.600 --> 01:12:14.600
So if you go to google.com, in fact, let's just go ahead and do that in the browser.

01:12:14.600 --> 01:12:19.600
Go to google.com, we are on the search page, right?

01:12:19.600 --> 01:12:31.600
If we search for hello world, we'll get a page with many different search results for the hello world query.

01:12:31.600 --> 01:12:35.600
And if we look in the URL up here at the top,

01:12:35.600 --> 01:12:44.600
we can actually see that that first query parameter is q equals hello plus world.

01:12:44.600 --> 01:12:54.600
So Google is actually taking our search and using it in the URL of this results page as the query parameter, right?

01:12:54.600 --> 01:13:01.600
So we could actually navigate directly to the hello world query by copying just that section of the URL, right?

01:13:01.600 --> 01:13:07.600
With the q equals hello plus world, and we'd go directly to those search results.

01:13:07.600 --> 01:13:09.600
So again, we're kind of on the same page.

01:13:09.600 --> 01:13:14.600
We're still on the Google home page where we can search for things,

01:13:14.600 --> 01:13:20.600
but they're using the query parameter to specify what term is being searched for.

01:13:20.600 --> 01:13:26.600
So let's break down this URL that we were just on, right?

01:13:26.600 --> 01:13:34.600
HTTPS, it's the protocol, www.google.com, the domain, slash search.

01:13:34.600 --> 01:13:36.600
It's the path, right?

01:13:36.600 --> 01:13:44.600
And Google is kind of using slash search as the results page and then using the query parameter,

01:13:44.600 --> 01:13:52.600
which is this question mark q equals hello plus world to specify which term is being searched for.

01:13:52.600 --> 01:13:54.600
If you think about it, this makes a lot of sense.

01:13:54.600 --> 01:13:58.600
There's an infinite number of things we could search for on Google.

01:13:58.600 --> 01:14:01.600
So using a path might not make a ton of sense, right?

01:14:01.600 --> 01:14:08.600
We don't really have a separate or new page for every possible search query, right?

01:14:08.600 --> 01:14:17.600
So they have one page, the search page, and they just change the query parameter based on the term that's being searched for.

01:14:17.600 --> 01:14:29.600
So this syntax, the question mark, separates the rest of the URL from the query parameters.

01:14:29.600 --> 01:14:31.600
And then we have key value pairs.

01:14:31.600 --> 01:14:35.600
So in this case, the key is q, the value is hello world.

01:14:35.600 --> 01:14:44.600
There could be more key value pairs after the q equals hello world, and they would be separated by ampersands.

01:14:44.600 --> 01:14:50.600
So an ampersand is the, if you look at your keyboard, it's the shift, shift version of seven, right?

01:14:50.600 --> 01:14:52.600
Hold shift, press seven, you get an ampersand.

01:14:52.600 --> 01:14:58.600
So we could do q equals hello plus world ampersand, maybe filter equals seven or something, right?

01:14:58.600 --> 01:15:02.600
We could just do key value pairs in the rest of the URL.

01:15:02.600 --> 01:15:06.600
We'll get to that more in detail later.

01:15:06.600 --> 01:15:10.600
So the question is, query parameters are required in a URL.

01:15:10.600 --> 01:15:12.600
That is false.

01:15:12.600 --> 01:15:18.600
Okay, so this next question is in a website, query parameters rarely change blank.

01:15:18.600 --> 01:15:24.600
The answers are which page is served, marketing and analytics information, or the contents of the page.

01:15:24.600 --> 01:15:32.600
So we talked about how query parameters typically don't change something big like the resource that's being requested or the web page itself.

01:15:32.600 --> 01:15:37.600
They typically change something smaller like options or contents, right?

01:15:37.600 --> 01:15:45.600
So for the example of Google, the page didn't change, we were still on the results page when we changed the query parameter.

01:15:45.600 --> 01:15:53.600
But the search term that we were looking for results for did change when the query parameter changed.

01:15:53.600 --> 01:15:56.600
So it's definitely not contents of the page, right?

01:15:56.600 --> 01:16:00.600
Additionally, marketing and analytics information, I think we can rule that out.

01:16:00.600 --> 01:16:06.600
Query parameters are very often used to do like affiliate link tracking, commission tracking, that sort of thing.

01:16:06.600 --> 01:16:12.600
Which page is served though, that rarely changes when the query parameters changes.

01:16:12.600 --> 01:16:15.600
Usually the page is dependent upon the path component.

01:16:15.600 --> 01:16:19.600
So I'm going to go with which page is served.

01:16:19.600 --> 01:16:23.600
Let's talk about synchronous and asynchronous code.

01:16:23.600 --> 01:16:26.600
To start, let's jump right into synchronous code.

01:16:26.600 --> 01:16:32.600
So these three console log statements are a great example of synchronous code, right?

01:16:32.600 --> 01:16:34.600
Synchronous code is simple.

01:16:34.600 --> 01:16:37.600
It's what we usually work with and we do it for good reason.

01:16:37.600 --> 01:16:39.600
It's simple and predictable, right?

01:16:39.600 --> 01:16:44.600
If I were to run this code here, I'd be printing first, I print first.

01:16:44.600 --> 01:16:46.600
Next, I would log, I print second.

01:16:46.600 --> 01:16:49.600
And next, I would log, I print third, right?

01:16:49.600 --> 01:16:56.600
We call this synchronous code because it's just happening in the order that each kind of line of code is called.

01:16:56.600 --> 01:16:59.600
There's no fuzzy timings or anything happening.

01:16:59.600 --> 01:17:02.600
It's very predictable, very straightforward.

01:17:02.600 --> 01:17:06.600
The other kind of processing we could do is asynchronous processing.

01:17:06.600 --> 01:17:12.600
Asynchronous processing is a little bit more complex, which is why we only use it when we need to.

01:17:12.600 --> 01:17:19.600
But in a nutshell, asynchronous processing allows us to do multiple things at the same time.

01:17:19.600 --> 01:17:23.600
So for example, if we need to render something in the browser,

01:17:23.600 --> 01:17:29.600
but at the same time we want to go fetch some data from a remote server by an HTTP call,

01:17:29.600 --> 01:17:33.600
we can do that asynchronously so that the browser can do its work

01:17:33.600 --> 01:17:39.600
while we're also waiting on a server to send us back some information.

