01:53:37.600 --> 01:53:48.600
Let's talk about the difference between bugs, bugs and errors in code.

01:53:48.600 --> 01:53:51.600
So bugs and errors are not the same.

01:53:51.600 --> 01:53:54.600
When I first started writing code, I was kind of fuzzy on this whole idea.

01:53:54.600 --> 01:53:59.600
And to be honest, I think for a long time, I assumed they were just kind of the same thing.

01:53:59.600 --> 01:54:06.600
In reality, bugs are bad, as you probably know.

01:54:06.600 --> 01:54:15.600
The interesting thing is that errors are not always bad, not always bad.

01:54:15.600 --> 01:54:22.600
Okay, as we've defined them, bugs are kind of unexpected things that are happening in our code.

01:54:22.600 --> 01:54:27.600
So we want our code to do something, right, hopefully something useful.

01:54:27.600 --> 01:54:32.600
And if it's not doing that thing, then we say we have a bug in our code.

01:54:32.600 --> 01:54:38.600
So bugs are things that are unexpected.

01:54:38.600 --> 01:54:44.600
To give an example, let's say that we had a function that we had a function in our code that adds two numbers,

01:54:44.600 --> 01:54:50.600
something like add, and it takes x and y as inputs, and it produces some result.

01:54:50.600 --> 01:54:53.600
We'll just call it z.

01:54:53.600 --> 01:55:05.600
If we called add with two and three, and we got the result of seven, we would say we have a bug, right?

01:55:05.600 --> 01:55:12.600
We expected the add function when given two and three to produce a five, but we are getting a seven instead.

01:55:12.600 --> 01:55:16.600
That's a bug. It's something that's unexpected in our code.

01:55:16.600 --> 01:55:23.600
An error, on the other hand, is very often expected, expected.

01:55:23.600 --> 01:55:29.600
And our job as programmers is to handle that error gracefully.

01:55:29.600 --> 01:55:34.600
So the way we approach fixing bugs and errors is quite different, right?

01:55:34.600 --> 01:55:39.600
When we have a bug, really, the only thing we can do is go fix the code, right?

01:55:39.600 --> 01:55:42.600
We actually have to go fix our application.

01:55:42.600 --> 01:55:48.600
Now, to be fair, there are certain circumstances where fixing a bug might be too expensive or maybe the bug is there,

01:55:48.600 --> 01:55:51.600
but it's actually not affecting many of the users, so we can kind of ignore it for now.

01:55:51.600 --> 01:55:55.600
That's more of a business and cost decision, right?

01:55:55.600 --> 01:56:00.600
In a perfect world with unlimited resources, of course, we would always fix all bugs.

01:56:00.600 --> 01:56:08.600
But the point that I'm trying to get across is when there's a bug, really, all you can do is fix the code.

01:56:08.600 --> 01:56:12.600
Errors, on the other hand, sometimes are unavoidable.

01:56:12.600 --> 01:56:17.600
You can't actually fix the code to remove errors.

01:56:17.600 --> 01:56:29.600
So, for example, an error that you might get in your code is there is no internet, no internet connection.

01:56:29.600 --> 01:56:34.600
So if you're writing a web app that needs to communicate over the internet to get its job done

01:56:34.600 --> 01:56:42.600
and the user's computer has been disconnected from the internet, there's really nothing in your code that you can do to fix that, right?

01:56:42.600 --> 01:56:46.600
You can't magically bring back an internet connection.

01:56:46.600 --> 01:56:50.600
So all you can really do is handle that error gracefully, right?

01:56:50.600 --> 01:56:53.600
Maybe by showing a message to the user.

01:56:53.600 --> 01:57:00.600
Another common error might be something like the remote, the remote server.

01:57:00.600 --> 01:57:03.600
Server is down.

01:57:03.600 --> 01:57:11.600
So you're writing some code on one machine that, again, maybe reaches out over a network to a server or a database.

01:57:11.600 --> 01:57:14.600
And while you have internet connection, maybe that server is offline.

01:57:14.600 --> 01:57:19.600
So that's another common type of error that crops up that, again, nothing's wrong with your code,

01:57:19.600 --> 01:57:25.600
but it's something that you need to be able to handle and maybe display to the user, something like that.

01:57:25.600 --> 01:57:29.600
Another common error would be user input.

01:57:29.600 --> 01:57:32.600
Bad user input, right?

01:57:32.600 --> 01:57:38.600
So maybe you're writing an application that requires users to have passwords over 12 characters, right?

01:57:38.600 --> 01:57:44.600
When a user puts in a 10-character password, we might consider that an error.

01:57:44.600 --> 01:57:50.600
Again, it doesn't mean there's anything wrong with our code, but we need to handle that error and maybe display some message to the user.

01:57:50.600 --> 01:57:58.600
One final note on the distinction between bugs and errors is that bugs have to be fixed in code.

01:57:58.600 --> 01:58:02.600
Errors have to be handled in code, right?

01:58:02.600 --> 01:58:04.600
So we expect errors. We must handle them.

01:58:04.600 --> 01:58:10.600
If we don't handle our errors properly, they may result in bugs.

01:58:10.600 --> 01:58:14.600
So like we just talked about, bugs and errors are not the same, right?

01:58:14.600 --> 01:58:20.600
Debugging is the process by which we remove bugs from the code, right?

01:58:20.600 --> 01:58:24.600
And then error handling is how we handle errors in our code.

01:58:24.600 --> 01:58:27.600
They're two very different and distinct things.

01:58:27.600 --> 01:58:30.600
Try-catch blocks are not a debugging tool.

01:58:30.600 --> 01:58:33.600
They're not ways to find bugs in our code, right?

01:58:33.600 --> 01:58:38.600
They're a way to handle errors at runtime, things like the internet connection being down.

01:58:38.600 --> 01:58:48.600
The whole takeaway from this is do not try to use try-catch for debugging and do not try to use a debugger to introduce error handling in your application.

01:58:48.600 --> 01:58:51.600
Understand those are two very different things.

01:58:51.600 --> 01:58:59.600
Okay, so the question that goes along with this is the presence of which of the following indicates that the developer has something to fix.

01:58:59.600 --> 01:59:02.600
Bugs, errors.

01:59:02.600 --> 01:59:06.600
Well, errors are always going to be present.

01:59:06.600 --> 01:59:14.600
Unless you have the most simple app, right, that doesn't do anything with a network or anything with user input, there will always be some sort of error handling.

01:59:14.600 --> 01:59:17.600
So I'm going to go with bugs.

01:59:17.600 --> 01:59:22.600
Next question is you should use error handling techniques to fix bugs.

01:59:22.600 --> 01:59:27.600
No, you should use a debugger or debugging techniques, right?

01:59:27.600 --> 01:59:33.600
Log statements, print statements, things like that to find bugs and remove them from your code.

01:59:33.600 --> 01:59:37.600
