02:47:18.600 --> 02:47:22.600
Let's talk about CRUD.

02:47:22.600 --> 02:47:25.600
So CRUD is an acronym.

02:47:25.600 --> 02:47:30.600
Stands for create, read, update and delete.

02:47:30.600 --> 02:47:31.600
Create.

02:47:31.600 --> 02:47:34.600
I'll do these. Let me do these in different colors.

02:47:34.600 --> 02:47:36.600
Create.

02:47:36.600 --> 02:47:40.600
Read.

02:47:40.600 --> 02:47:49.600
Update.

02:47:49.600 --> 02:47:55.600
So when we're dealing with a web server, pretty much every action we take,

02:47:55.600 --> 02:48:01.600
nearly every action we take when interacting with a server is one of these actions.

02:48:01.600 --> 02:48:04.600
We either want to create some new resource on the server, right?

02:48:04.600 --> 02:48:09.600
Create a new user account, create a new weapon in a video game, right?

02:48:09.600 --> 02:48:11.600
Create a new tweet.

02:48:11.600 --> 02:48:14.600
We want to read something, right?

02:48:14.600 --> 02:48:19.600
Read a tweet, read a list of tweets, see which of our friends are online.

02:48:19.600 --> 02:48:21.600
We want to update something, right?

02:48:21.600 --> 02:48:25.600
Maybe update a password or we want to delete something, delete an account, et cetera.

02:48:25.600 --> 02:48:31.600
Like I said, almost all actions fall into one of these four buckets, right?

02:48:31.600 --> 02:48:34.600
Create, read, update, delete.

02:48:34.600 --> 02:48:41.600
As it happens, these actions map very simply to the HTTP methods.

02:48:41.600 --> 02:48:47.600
So create is a post HTTP method.

02:48:47.600 --> 02:48:54.600
Read, this is the one we've already covered, is a get.

02:48:54.600 --> 02:49:00.600
Update is going to be a put.

02:49:00.600 --> 02:49:06.600
And I'll just like special mention, sometimes it's a patch,

02:49:06.600 --> 02:49:11.600
but I would underscore it's usually a put.

02:49:11.600 --> 02:49:22.600
And then delete is the easiest one because it's delete HTTP method.

02:49:22.600 --> 02:49:25.600
Okay, so when people talk about CRUD servers,

02:49:25.600 --> 02:49:28.600
they're talking about a web server that allows the users of the API

02:49:28.600 --> 02:49:31.600
to create, read, update, and delete resources.

02:49:31.600 --> 02:49:36.600
And then typically, they will use these HTTP methods to do it.

02:49:36.600 --> 02:49:40.600
Now, I want to point out, this is a convention.

02:49:40.600 --> 02:49:45.600
This is a convention, this mapping, right?

02:49:45.600 --> 02:49:52.600
Technically, there's no reason a server can't use get requests to create resources.

02:49:52.600 --> 02:49:56.600
There's nothing stopping the server from being designed that way.

02:49:56.600 --> 02:50:03.600
However, the convention in HTTP, specifically kind of RESTful servers,

02:50:03.600 --> 02:50:05.600
which is something we'll get into later,

02:50:05.600 --> 02:50:12.600
is to map kind of the create, read, update, and delete to these specific methods.

02:50:12.600 --> 02:50:15.600
And it's just so that it makes it easier on us, right?

02:50:15.600 --> 02:50:17.600
We get used to doing things this way.

02:50:17.600 --> 02:50:22.600
And then when we're working with APIs, which every API is different and complicated,

02:50:22.600 --> 02:50:26.600
because we're using something familiar, it makes it easier to interact with.

02:50:26.600 --> 02:50:30.600
So again, this is a mapping that you don't necessarily need to memorize it now,

02:50:30.600 --> 02:50:34.600
but it's something that you definitely will get really familiar with over time

02:50:34.600 --> 02:50:37.600
as you work with more and more servers, and it will become second nature.

02:50:37.600 --> 02:50:40.600
Okay, so now we've got a question that goes along with all of that,

02:50:40.600 --> 02:50:45.600
create, read, update, delete, kind of mapping to HTTP method stuff.

02:50:45.600 --> 02:50:53.600
So the question is, HTTP methods map to CRUD actions by convention, automatically, or always?

02:50:53.600 --> 02:50:55.600
The answer is by convention.

02:50:55.600 --> 02:51:01.600
Next question is, to update a user account, I would probably use a blank request.

02:51:01.600 --> 02:51:03.600
The answer is going to be put.

02:51:03.600 --> 02:51:11.600
So an HTTP post request sends data to a server typically to create some new resource on the server.

02:51:11.600 --> 02:51:15.600
And the way that differs from a get request we can see down here.

02:51:15.600 --> 02:51:20.600
Well, there's actually a couple things. So first of all, the method is post, one should be fairly obvious.

02:51:20.600 --> 02:51:24.600
But additionally, we need to send a content type header.

02:51:24.600 --> 02:51:27.600
Not every server is going to require it.

02:51:27.600 --> 02:51:36.600
But it's definitely best practice to always inform the entity on the other side of a fetch or an HTTP request,

02:51:36.600 --> 02:51:41.600
the kind of data that we're sending in the body, right, so they don't try to parse it the wrong way.

02:51:41.600 --> 02:51:43.600
So in this case, we're going to be sending JSON data.

02:51:43.600 --> 02:51:48.600
So we should specify in the headers that we are sending JSON data.

02:51:48.600 --> 02:51:51.600
And then finally is the inclusion of a body.

02:51:51.600 --> 02:51:57.600
So when we were getting data from a server, we didn't need to send any data in the body.

02:51:57.600 --> 02:51:58.600
We could leave it empty. Right.

02:51:58.600 --> 02:52:05.600
But now that we are actually sending some information to the server so that that information can be saved on the server,

02:52:05.600 --> 02:52:06.600
we need to specify that in the body.

02:52:06.600 --> 02:52:12.600
It's also important to point out that those requests are not safe in the same way that get requests are.

02:52:12.600 --> 02:52:15.600
Get requests are very safe inherently, right.

02:52:15.600 --> 02:52:17.600
Nothing's changing on the server.

02:52:17.600 --> 02:52:23.600
Typically, we're just asking for kind of copies of information that's stored there.

02:52:23.600 --> 02:52:27.600
Post requests are altering the state of the server in some form or another.

02:52:27.600 --> 02:52:32.600
There are strategies that servers can use to kind of deduplicate.

02:52:32.600 --> 02:52:37.600
Say you were to accidentally send the same post request twice to create a new user.

02:52:37.600 --> 02:52:42.600
Smarter servers can can kind of deduplicate that and make sure only one entity is created.

02:52:42.600 --> 02:52:44.600
But that's not always the case.

02:52:44.600 --> 02:52:50.600
So it's just important to keep in mind that because post requests are kind of mutating data on the server,

02:52:50.600 --> 02:52:54.600
they're changing something that's stored there.

02:52:54.600 --> 02:52:56.600
You do need to just be a little more careful with them.

