02:56:15.600 --> 02:56:19.600
Let's talk about status codes.

02:56:19.600 --> 02:56:23.600
HTTP status codes.

02:56:23.600 --> 02:56:28.600
So status codes are basically one additional thing that we need to check

02:56:28.600 --> 02:56:32.600
to ensure that an HTTP request went the way that we hope it did, right?

02:56:32.600 --> 02:56:34.600
That everything went well.

02:56:34.600 --> 02:56:39.600
The first thing that we typically check, right, is if an error was produced.

02:56:39.600 --> 02:56:41.600
And we've already talked about errors.

02:56:41.600 --> 02:56:49.600
Status codes are kind of the second level, the second layer that we need to pass

02:56:49.600 --> 02:56:54.600
in order to make sure that, again, the request kind of went off without a hitch.

02:56:54.600 --> 02:57:00.600
Let's diagram the lifecycle of a fetch request so that we can see kind of how status codes

02:57:00.600 --> 02:57:06.600
and errors differ and when you might get one versus the other.

02:57:06.600 --> 02:57:09.600
So over here, I'm going to draw the client.

02:57:09.600 --> 02:57:11.600
And on this side, I'll have the server.

02:57:11.600 --> 02:57:16.600
You're probably super familiar with this diagram I'm going to draw by now.

02:57:16.600 --> 02:57:21.600
This will be the HTTP request going out.

02:57:21.600 --> 02:57:26.600
And this will be the HTTP response back.

02:57:26.600 --> 02:57:29.600
Okay.

02:57:29.600 --> 02:57:40.600
If something goes wrong in this area, it will produce, most likely produce an error.

02:57:40.600 --> 02:57:47.600
So for example, if we don't give a valid domain name to the fetch API, we might get an error.

02:57:47.600 --> 02:57:51.600
Or if the internet connection is down, so we're not able to complete the request,

02:57:51.600 --> 02:57:55.600
then we would probably get an error from the fetch API.

02:57:55.600 --> 02:58:00.600
Okay. If something goes wrong on the server.

02:58:00.600 --> 02:58:03.600
So this means our request successfully made it to the server.

02:58:03.600 --> 02:58:06.600
And now the server is processing the request.

02:58:06.600 --> 02:58:13.600
If something goes wrong during the process of the request, then that results in a status code.

02:58:13.600 --> 02:58:17.600
And we will still get a valid HTTP response back.

02:58:17.600 --> 02:58:22.600
The status code is contained in in the response.

02:58:22.600 --> 02:58:23.600
Okay.

02:58:23.600 --> 02:58:26.600
So if something goes wrong on the server and the server detects it,

02:58:26.600 --> 02:58:31.600
it will let us know in the HTTP response via a status code.

02:58:31.600 --> 02:58:35.600
So this might be something like, hey, you don't have permission to access that resource.

02:58:35.600 --> 02:58:38.600
That you're requesting, right?

02:58:38.600 --> 02:58:41.600
Or one of our backend services is down.

02:58:41.600 --> 02:58:42.600
We're not able to process that request.

02:58:42.600 --> 02:58:46.600
Those are the kinds of things that you'll see in status codes.

02:58:46.600 --> 02:58:52.600
So another one of the big differences between status codes and errors are that errors only occur if something goes wrong.

02:58:52.600 --> 02:58:56.600
Whereas every HTTP response has a status code.

02:58:56.600 --> 02:59:01.600
We just need to check what the status code is to see if something actually went wrong.

02:59:01.600 --> 02:59:07.600
So the codes are broken up into kind of different error levels by by number.

02:59:07.600 --> 02:59:12.600
So status codes are between 100 and 599.

02:59:12.600 --> 02:59:16.600
And basically 100 level codes are informational responses.

02:59:16.600 --> 02:59:18.600
Frankly, they're very rare.

02:59:18.600 --> 02:59:20.600
You won't see these very often.

02:59:20.600 --> 02:59:23.600
200 level codes are hopefully what you see the most often.

02:59:23.600 --> 02:59:25.600
And they're success codes.

02:59:25.600 --> 02:59:27.600
So 200 is the famous.

02:59:27.600 --> 02:59:28.600
Everything's OK.

02:59:28.600 --> 02:59:31.600
You'll see 200 quite often.

02:59:31.600 --> 02:59:34.600
300 are redirection messages.

02:59:34.600 --> 02:59:37.600
So they're basically telling the client, hey, the server moved.

02:59:37.600 --> 02:59:39.600
You need to redirect your quest to somewhere else.

02:59:39.600 --> 02:59:53.600
If you've ever been to a link, like if you ever visited a link that doesn't actually end up taking you to the URL in your browser that you visually saw, you likely were redirected through a 300 level status code.

02:59:53.600 --> 02:59:55.600
And then 400 are client errors.

02:59:55.600 --> 02:59:59.600
So it's basically the server telling the client, hey, you're not authenticated.

02:59:59.600 --> 03:00:00.600
You don't have permissions.

03:00:00.600 --> 03:00:03.600
You formatted something improperly.

03:00:03.600 --> 03:00:06.600
And then 500 level errors are server errors.

03:00:06.600 --> 03:00:09.600
So it's actually the server saying, hey, something messed up on our end.

03:00:09.600 --> 03:00:12.600
Maybe our database is down or something like that.

03:00:12.600 --> 03:00:16.600
So let's take a look here at some of the more common status codes that you'll see.

03:00:16.600 --> 03:00:18.600
So 200 is just OK.

03:00:18.600 --> 03:00:22.600
Right. This is by far the most common code and basically just means your request worked.

03:00:22.600 --> 03:00:24.600
Right. You can move on.

03:00:24.600 --> 03:00:26.600
201 is just a little bit more specific.

03:00:26.600 --> 03:00:30.600
So it's saying everything went well, but specifically you created something.

03:00:30.600 --> 03:00:36.600
Right. So 201 is often the response to a post request when a resource is created on the server.

03:00:36.600 --> 03:00:38.600
301 moved permanently.

03:00:38.600 --> 03:00:44.600
So this means, hey, the server that handled this type of request used to live here.

03:00:44.600 --> 03:00:46.600
Now it lives somewhere else.

