Sometimes you'll see an endpoint where you are able to give it data via a put request.

03:04:56.600 --> 03:05:02.600
And the action on the server is to create it if it doesn't exist or to update it if it does exist.

03:05:02.600 --> 03:05:05.600
That would typically be done with a put request.

03:05:05.600 --> 03:05:10.600
So the main difference between post and put is that post is almost always just creating.

03:05:10.600 --> 03:05:16.600
Whereas put is like update or create or just update.

03:05:16.600 --> 03:05:22.600
Another big difference is that put is meant to be idempotent or idempotent.

03:05:22.600 --> 03:05:24.600
I don't know how to pronounce that.

03:05:24.600 --> 03:05:32.600
But basically what it means is you should be able to send a put request multiple times without creating multiple resources.

03:05:32.600 --> 03:05:36.600
Effectively, the you send the put request the first time and it makes an update.

03:05:36.600 --> 03:05:38.600
If you send the identical put request, nothing happens.

03:05:38.600 --> 03:05:41.600
It's a no op or a no operation.

03:05:41.600 --> 03:05:45.600
So put requests should be safer, generally speaking, than post requests.

03:05:45.600 --> 03:05:54.600
And the reason they're able to be idempotent, again, generally speaking, is because you'll often be including the ID of the resource that you're updating.

03:05:54.600 --> 03:06:00.600
Whereas with a post request, you'll often be kind of relying on the server to generate new IDs.

03:06:00.600 --> 03:06:05.600
So that makes sense. If you make multiple post requests, you're generating new IDs for new resources.

03:06:05.600 --> 03:06:08.600
Whereas if you're making put requests, you're always using that same ID.

03:06:08.600 --> 03:06:11.600
So you just be updating fields you already updated.

