03:21:55.600 --> 03:22:02.600
So you've heard me toss around the term RESTful so far in this course, probably. But I haven't really explained what it is yet.

03:22:02.600 --> 03:22:06.600
REST is an acronym, stands for Representational State Transfer.

03:22:06.600 --> 03:22:15.600
But all it really is is a set of standards and conventions for building better APIs. At least that's the promise, right?

03:22:15.600 --> 03:22:32.600
So again, the whole goal of a RESTful API is to adhere to certain rules so that a client, right, us in this case, that's interacting with the API, kind of knows what to expect and has a really good developer experience working with that API.

03:22:32.600 --> 03:22:42.600
So what are the rules and conventions that RESTful servers adhere to that kind of separate them from other kinds of servers, other kinds of APIs?

03:22:42.600 --> 03:22:54.600
Well, the first is that one of the kind of core tenets of a RESTful API is that the two pieces, the client and the server, are kind of separate and agnostic, right?

03:22:54.600 --> 03:23:01.600
So the client can be written in any programming language. The server can be written in a different programming, right?

03:23:01.600 --> 03:23:13.600
There's kind of a minimalist set of things that the two need to agree on, things like, you know, what names are we going to use for resources? Are we going to use JSON or the encoding scheme?

03:23:13.600 --> 03:23:22.600
But everything else can be completely separate and distinct. Another key tenet of RESTful APIs is statelessness.

03:23:22.600 --> 03:23:33.600
So the server will have its state and the client will have its state. But in a good RESTful server, you won't have kind of persistent state that deals with the connection between the two.

03:23:33.600 --> 03:23:43.600
Let me give an example. So on the server, let's just say it's the FantasyQuest server and we have 100 registered players in the game.

03:23:43.600 --> 03:23:53.600
Well, then one player deletes their account. Now there's 99. So the server is inherently stateful, right? It has state, it's keeping track of state, and that's totally okay.

03:23:53.600 --> 03:24:05.600
There's really no way to get around that. What's interesting about REST is that the client and the server shouldn't be keeping track of, like, past interactions that they've had.

03:24:05.600 --> 03:24:16.600
So, for example, if the client says, give me the first 10 users, and it gets 10 user records, and then it makes the same requests, the same request, excuse me,

03:24:16.600 --> 03:24:24.600
the server shouldn't remember that it already gave that client 10 users and give them the next 10 users. It shouldn't do that.

03:24:24.600 --> 03:24:31.600
What should have to happen is the client specifically asks for the next 10 users. So now it's a separate request.

03:24:31.600 --> 03:24:39.600
And basically what that means is the server doesn't have to keep track of any state. It doesn't have to remember, hey, this client already asked me for some stuff.

03:24:39.600 --> 03:24:54.600
I'm going to change the way I respond. It's just a very clean way to write an API that kind of puts some responsibility on the client to ask for things in, like, new and different ways when it needs new and different information.

03:24:54.600 --> 03:25:05.600
Another key component of RESTful servers that really sets them apart from other types of servers is that the path is used to designate the resource that's being interacted with.

03:25:05.600 --> 03:25:15.600
So whenever you're working with a RESTful server, you should be able to hit, say, the slash locations endpoint, if there is a locations resource available on the server,

03:25:15.600 --> 03:25:26.600
and be able to create, read, update and delete that resource in a predictable way, right, just by by making requests to that path with the appropriate HTTP method.

03:25:26.600 --> 03:25:37.600
So the question is a true false statement. And it says RESTful servers communicate the state of resources. They don't expose access to arbitrary server commands.

03:25:37.600 --> 03:25:47.600
So this is true, right? REST is all about state transfer, just transferring the state from the client to the server, from the server to the client, right?

03:25:47.600 --> 03:25:57.600
Both are staying in sync with each other by making requests back and forth. Arbitrary commands are not allowed.

03:25:57.600 --> 03:26:08.600
Next question, does a backend server need to adhere to RESTful standards? The answers are no, but it's a common convention designed to create better API's. Yes, otherwise the requests will fail.

03:26:08.600 --> 03:26:13.600
Yes, if it doesn't, it's an insecure server. And no, it's generally a bad idea.

03:26:13.600 --> 03:26:23.600
Well, the answer is definitely one of the no answers, right? REST is just an option. A server or an API doesn't need to use it.

03:26:23.600 --> 03:26:30.600
So that narrows it down to it's generally a bad idea. And it's a common convention designed to create better API's. Well, it's not generally a bad idea.

03:26:30.600 --> 03:26:39.600
REST is very popular and it's popular for a reason. I wouldn't go so far as to say REST is better or REST should always be used.

03:26:39.600 --> 03:26:49.600
There are other popular conventions, things like GraphQL, for example, if you've ever heard of that, completely ignore all of the RESTful conventions and do things a completely different way.

03:26:49.600 --> 03:26:58.600
But I will say it's this top one. It's a common convention and it is designed to help create better API's.

