03:36:33.600 --> 03:36:36.600
So you may have been wondering, isn't this an HTTP course?

03:36:36.600 --> 03:36:43.600
Why is every request in the course kind of prefixed with HTTPS instead of HTTP?

03:36:43.600 --> 03:36:47.600
Well, the reason is HTTPS is essentially the exact same thing as HTTP

03:36:47.600 --> 03:36:51.600
with one added component, and that is security.

03:36:51.600 --> 03:36:55.600
The most important security feature of HTTPS is encryption.

03:36:55.600 --> 03:36:59.600
It keeps the information that we are sending in our request

03:36:59.600 --> 03:37:03.600
and the information coming back in the response secure and encrypted

03:37:03.600 --> 03:37:07.600
so that only us as the sender and the server as the receiver

03:37:07.600 --> 03:37:10.600
are able to decrypt and process that information.

03:37:10.600 --> 03:37:13.600
When we send HTTP requests over the internet,

03:37:13.600 --> 03:37:18.600
they don't go directly from our computer to the server that we are communicating with.

03:37:18.600 --> 03:37:23.600
There are intermediaries, for example, your internet service provider, right?

03:37:23.600 --> 03:37:26.600
Or if you're working on public Wi-Fi, like within a coffee shop,

03:37:26.600 --> 03:37:30.600
then whoever owns that Wi-Fi or that router would be able to

03:37:30.600 --> 03:37:34.600
intercept your network traffic and process it.

03:37:34.600 --> 03:37:40.600
So if you're using plain HTTP, the data that you're transmitting is never safe

03:37:40.600 --> 03:37:42.600
in the process of being transmitted.

03:37:42.600 --> 03:37:46.600
Anyone in the middle can look at everything you're sending.

03:37:46.600 --> 03:37:48.600
Take the example of an e-commerce website.

03:37:48.600 --> 03:37:52.600
If you're entering in a credit card in your browser and sending it off to some server

03:37:52.600 --> 03:37:55.600
through a normal HTTP request,

03:37:55.600 --> 03:38:00.600
every intermediary now has access to your credit card information.

03:38:00.600 --> 03:38:06.600
That's why good e-commerce sites use HTTPS and should never use anything less secure.

