Let's talk about HTTPS and specifically its encryption.

03:38:54.600 --> 03:38:59.600
So how it keeps our data safe on the Internet.

03:38:59.600 --> 03:39:03.600
Start with a little diagram, one that you're probably very familiar with.

03:39:03.600 --> 03:39:07.600
So over here, we'll draw the client.

03:39:07.600 --> 03:39:12.600
And over here, the server.

03:39:12.600 --> 03:39:18.600
The problem that HTTPS solves is when we send an HTTP request across the Internet,

03:39:18.600 --> 03:39:22.600
there are potentially intermediaries here in the middle.

03:39:22.600 --> 03:39:26.600
Draw them in.

03:39:26.600 --> 03:39:30.600
For example, maybe this is the public Wi-Fi that you're using.

03:39:30.600 --> 03:39:35.600
It's the controller of that router and see your Internet traffic.

03:39:35.600 --> 03:39:39.600
Another one might be your ISP.

03:39:39.600 --> 03:39:44.600
Another one might be the cloud provider of the server.

03:39:44.600 --> 03:39:55.600
There's potentially many different entities that your Internet traffic is passing through on the way from your computer to the server.

03:39:55.600 --> 03:40:02.600
So in order to stop these different entities from being able to see all of the information that we are communicating privately with the server,

03:40:02.600 --> 03:40:07.600
we want to encrypt it. What that means is we need to encrypt.

03:40:07.600 --> 03:40:11.600
We need to encrypt over here on the client before sending the data.

