This blue one is the public key. Public.

03:40:45.600 --> 03:40:51.600
And we'll do the private key. Red.

03:40:51.600 --> 03:40:55.600
Public and private.

03:40:55.600 --> 03:41:00.600
Now, the public key is used for encrypting.

03:41:00.600 --> 03:41:04.600
It's used for encrypting information.

03:41:04.600 --> 03:41:11.600
The private key is used for decrypting.

03:41:11.600 --> 03:41:17.600
It's important to understand that the public key can encrypt information, but it cannot decrypt it.

03:41:17.600 --> 03:41:20.600
That's solely the responsibility of the private key.

03:41:20.600 --> 03:41:29.600
The private key stays on the server, while the public key can actually be given to the client.

03:41:29.600 --> 03:41:36.600
Now, this would be good enough, right? Using the public key, the client can encrypt some information and send it to the server.

03:41:36.600 --> 03:41:40.600
Unfortunately, there's another direction that we forgot about.

03:41:40.600 --> 03:41:46.600
We also need to be able to send private responses from the server back to the client.

03:41:46.600 --> 03:41:50.600
HTTP is a request response flow.

03:41:50.600 --> 03:41:58.600
So actually, instead of using this public key to encrypt and send the request,

03:41:58.600 --> 03:42:10.600
this asymmetric cryptography, which is what public-private key cryptography is called, is actually used to negotiate a third key.

03:42:10.600 --> 03:42:15.600
So we actually generate this third key.

03:42:15.600 --> 03:42:24.600
This is a symmetric encryption key.

03:42:24.600 --> 03:42:33.600
What this key does is it allows both parties using this single key to encrypt and decrypt messages.

03:42:33.600 --> 03:42:42.600
So once both the client and the server have this key, they can share messages back and forth and encrypt and decrypt both of them.

03:42:42.600 --> 03:42:53.600
This public-private key section is only used so that the client can communicate to the server privately and figure out what this key is going to be.

03:42:53.600 --> 03:42:58.600
This key is effectively generated on a per-request basis.

03:42:58.600 --> 03:43:04.600
Let's review the order in which all of this happens just to make sure it's crystal clear.

03:43:04.600 --> 03:43:15.600
The very first thing that happens is that the server generates public and private key.

03:43:15.600 --> 03:43:18.600
This only needs to happen once for the server.

03:43:18.600 --> 03:43:22.600
It's done when the server is very first configured for HTTPS.

03:43:22.600 --> 03:43:27.600
If the server doesn't have a public-private key pair, it's not going to be HTTPS enabled.

03:43:27.600 --> 03:43:30.600
It's not ready for HTTPS traffic.

03:43:30.600 --> 03:43:35.600
So this only needs to happen once per server.

03:43:35.600 --> 03:43:52.600
Then when an individual request is going to start, the client contacts the server and says, hey, I want to send you a secure request.

03:43:52.600 --> 03:44:03.600
At that point, the server sends the public key to the client.

03:44:03.600 --> 03:44:08.600
So this blue public key is getting sent over to the client.

03:44:08.600 --> 03:44:13.600
Next, the client encrypts...

03:44:13.600 --> 03:44:16.600
Well, actually, let's write it a slightly different way.

03:44:16.600 --> 03:44:25.600
Let's say the client and the server negotiate a symmetric key.

03:44:25.600 --> 03:44:31.600
So from a very high level, the way that this works is the client has the public key.

03:44:31.600 --> 03:44:34.600
So it's able to send encrypted messages to the server.

03:44:34.600 --> 03:44:39.600
But remember, the server cannot yet send encrypted messages back to the client.

03:44:39.600 --> 03:44:51.600
So the client generates a secret token, sends it to the server, and then both parties using that secret token are able to generate the same symmetric key.

03:44:51.600 --> 03:44:55.600
So again, public key comes from server to client.

03:44:55.600 --> 03:44:58.600
Client generates a secret token.

03:44:58.600 --> 03:45:04.600
The client sends that secret token encrypted with the public key to the server.

03:45:04.600 --> 03:45:06.600
The server decrypts it using the private key.

03:45:06.600 --> 03:45:16.600
And then both parties are able to generate the same symmetric key so that they can both use that key for encryption and decryption.

03:45:16.600 --> 03:45:33.600
So once that symmetric key has been negotiated, now the client sends an encrypted HTTP request.

03:45:33.600 --> 03:45:45.600
Then the server is able to decrypt the request.

03:45:45.600 --> 03:45:52.600
And the server sends an encrypted response.

03:45:52.600 --> 03:46:00.600
And finally, client is able to decrypt the response.

03:46:00.600 --> 03:46:02.600
And that's the whole lifecycle.

03:46:02.600 --> 03:46:08.600
So this exercise talks about all of that HTTPS stuff that we just went over on the blackboard.

03:46:08.600 --> 03:46:17.600
I will point out really quick before we answer this question that SSL and TLS, they're the kind of the security pieces of HTTPS.

03:46:17.600 --> 03:46:26.600
So you could think of HTTPS as SSL or TLS tacked on to HTTP in order to add security features.

03:46:26.600 --> 03:46:31.600
So the question is, HTTPS is essentially the same as HTTP with some additional security.

03:46:31.600 --> 03:46:33.600
And the answers are true or false.

03:46:33.600 --> 03:46:35.600
This one's going to be true.

03:46:35.600 --> 03:46:38.600
Basically the same as HTTP, we've just added some security.

03:46:38.600 --> 03:46:42.600
I want to cover two more key points when it comes to HTTPS.

03:46:42.600 --> 03:46:49.600
So we've talked about how HTTPS encrypts what it is that we are saying when we communicate online.

03:46:49.600 --> 03:46:54.600
So that people snooping in on our internet traffic can't read the content of our messages.

03:46:54.600 --> 03:46:56.600
And it does that very well.

03:46:56.600 --> 03:47:03.600
One thing that it does not do is protect your identity or the identity of who you are communicating with.

03:47:03.600 --> 03:47:11.600
So if someone is snooping in on your network traffic, even though you're using HTTPS, they will be able to see the website that you're browsing on.

03:47:11.600 --> 03:47:12.600
Right.

03:47:12.600 --> 03:47:21.600
So if you're on boot dev, for example, and you're using public Wi-Fi, whoever controls that public Wi-Fi will still be able to see that you're browsing boot dev.

03:47:21.600 --> 03:47:28.600
That being said, HTTPS does ensure that you're talking to who you think you're talking to.

03:47:28.600 --> 03:47:42.600
So if you're connected to boot dev over an HTTPS connection, then you can be sure that you're actually talking to boot dev and not some malicious hacker that's just pretending to be boot dev.

03:47:42.600 --> 03:47:47.600
That's enabled through digital signature technology, which is, again, something you can look up.

03:47:47.600 --> 03:47:49.600
It's kind of out of the scope of the course.

03:47:49.600 --> 03:47:56.600
It's just important to know that HTTPS does give you that assurity if you're using it.

03:47:56.600 --> 03:48:01.600
So the question is, HTTPS ensures that you know who you are communicating with.

03:48:01.600 --> 03:48:05.600
And as we just discussed, the answer is true.

03:48:05.600 --> 03:48:07.600
Next question is also a true false statement.

03:48:07.600 --> 03:48:11.600
It says HTTPS protects your identity online.

03:48:11.600 --> 03:48:20.600
And as we just talked about, it does encrypt our messages, but it doesn't really protect who we're communicating with or who we are.

03:48:20.600 --> 03:48:24.600
So the answer is going to be false on this one.

